        {
          "duration": {
            "from": 367.8,
            "to": 377.8
          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 2-year-old presents at night with barking cough, hoarse voice, and inspiratory</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 2-year-old presents at night with barking cough, hoarse voice, and inspiratory stridor following 2 days of coryza. The child is alert and interactive with no drooling or difficulty swallowing. Which feature of this presentation most reliably distinguishes croup from acute epiglottitis?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Presence of inspiratory stridor</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Inspiratory stridor occurs in both croup (subglottic narrowing) and epiglottitis (supraglottic swelling) and cannot distinguish between them.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>History of a 2-day viral prodrome</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A viral prodrome is typical of croup and uncommon in epiglottitis. However, the presence or absence of supraglottic signs — drooling, dysphagia, and forward posture — is what most reliably separates the two at the bedside.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Absence of drooling and dysphagia</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Drooling, difficulty swallowing, and a forward-leaning tripod posture indicate supraglottic obstruction seen in epiglottitis. Their absence in a child with barking cough and hoarseness strongly favours croup.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Nocturnal worsening of symptoms</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Nighttime worsening is characteristic of croup — recumbent secretion pooling at the subglottis combined with the circadian cortisol nadir — but does not by itself exclude epiglottitis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "In croup the child is alert and interactive with no difficulty swallowing. Epiglottitis causes drooling, dysphagia, and a forward-leaning posture. These supraglottic signs are the most reliable differentiators."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly. The key bedside distinction is that epiglottitis produces supraglottic signs — drooling, dysphagia, and an inability to lie flat — which are absent in croup."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
                "enableSolutionsButton": true,
