          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A child with moderate croup receives nebulised adrenaline in the emergency depar</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A child with moderate croup receives nebulised adrenaline in the emergency department and shows marked improvement within 15 minutes. What is the minimum observation period required before considering discharge?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>30 minutes</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Thirty minutes is insufficient. The vasoconstrictive effect of adrenaline begins to wane within 1–2 hours, and symptom rebound can occur by 2–3 hours.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>1 hour</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>One hour falls within the window where adrenaline is still active, so a child may appear well only to develop rebound stridor shortly after discharge.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>2 to 4 hours</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Nebulised adrenaline constricts subglottic mucosal blood vessels, easing breathing within minutes, but its effect wears off in 1–2 hours and symptoms can rebound at 2–3 hours. A 2–4 hour observation window ensures stability before discharge.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Overnight admission is always required</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Overnight admission is not routinely necessary. If the child remains well after the observation period and has received corticosteroids, safe discharge is appropriate.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Nebulised adrenaline provides rapid but temporary relief by constricting mucosal blood vessels. Its effect lasts only 1–2 hours and symptoms can rebound by 2–3 hours, so a minimum 2–4 hour observation is mandatory."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. The 2–4 hour observation window accounts for the transient vasoconstrictive effect of nebulised adrenaline and the risk of symptom rebound once it wears off."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
                "enableSolutionsButton": true,
                "enableCheckButton": true,
