          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 10-month-old being treated for pneumonia has persistent high fever after 48 ho</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 10-month-old being treated for pneumonia has persistent high fever after 48 hours of appropriate antibiotics, worsening respiratory distress, dullness to percussion, and absent breath sounds over the right lower zone. What is the most likely complication?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Bronchiolitis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Bronchiolitis is a primary viral infection of the bronchioles presenting with wheeze and fine crackles, not a complication of bacterial pneumonia. It does not cause dullness to percussion.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Pneumothorax</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Pneumothorax causes hyper-resonance on percussion, not dullness. The stony dullness in this case points to fluid in the pleural space, not air.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Empyema thoracis</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Persistent fever despite antibiotics, worsening distress, dullness to percussion, and absent breath sounds indicate infected pus collecting in the pleural space — the hallmark of empyema complicating pneumonia.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Wheeze-associated lower respiratory tract infection</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Wheeze-associated LRTI features bronchospasm with expiratory wheeze triggered by viral infection. It does not cause dullness to percussion or absent breath sounds, which indicate pleural fluid.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "When pneumonia fails to respond to antibiotics and signs of pleural fluid appear — dullness to percussion, absent breath sounds — suspect empyema thoracis, infected pus in the pleural space requiring drainage."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. Empyema thoracis is the key complication to suspect when a child with pneumonia fails to improve on antibiotics and develops signs of pleural fluid — dullness and absent breath sounds over one zone."
                }
              ],
